  "name": "VITAMIN B1  (THIAMINE)",
  "code": "1844",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul>\n<li>Vitamin B1 is increased in leukemia, polycythemia vera, and Hodgkin's disease.</li>\n<li>It is decreased in alcoholism with and without liver disease., in deficient diet, chronic febrile infections, prolonged diarrhoea, diabetes, carcinoid syndrome Hartnup disease, and pellagra.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical utility:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Measurement of Vitamin B1 is useful in the assessment of thiamine deficiency, in the investigation of suspected&nbsp;beriberi, and in monitoring the effects of chronic alcoholism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Limitations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs that may decrease vitamin B1 levels include glibenclamide, isoniazid, and valproic acid.</li>\n<li>Diets high in freshwater fish and tea, which are thiamine antagonists may cause decreased vitamin B1 levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Associated Tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>V0036-Vitamin B Complex profile, V0012-Vitamin B2 Riboflavin EDTA Whole Blood, V8123-Vitamin B3 (Niacin),</li>\n<li>V8125- Vitamin B5, V0013-Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine EDTA Whole Blood, V8124-Vitamin B7 (Biotin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kit Insert</li>\n<li>Wallach's Interpretation of Diagnostic tests-10th Edition</li>\n</ul>",
